# CLAUDE.md — standing rules. This file is the CARRIER. Do not rewrite it per revision.

THIS FILE CONTAINS NO MEASUREMENTS AND NO NUMBERS THAT CAN GO STALE. Every figure
in this project lives in `STATE.md` (machine-written) or `verify_clone.sh` (runs).
`verify_clone.sh`'s own header states the doctrine: "a number in a paragraph goes
stale silently ... a number in a script that runs cannot." Obey it here.

Rules 1-15 below are the subset of the 35-rule canon that applies to EVERY session
regardless of task. The full canon, and the narrow instrument-craft rules, are in
the imports at the bottom.

---

## Before you touch anything

**1. MEASURE THE MERGE STATE; NEVER TRANSCRIBE IT.** (canon rule 17.) The branch
instruction in the brief has been stale six revisions running. Run the ahead/behind
loop and `git diff --name-only HEAD...origin/main` — his photographs arrive on main
mid-revision without notice. Re-run that diff EVERY session, not once. `bootstrap.sh`
row 10, "no branch carries work HEAD does not have", outranks any sentence about
which branch to use, including this one.

**2. WHERE THE MACHINE AND THE PROSE DISAGREE, THE MACHINE IS RIGHT** — say so and
correct the prose in the same revision. Order of authority: the machine, then the
owner, then the record. Before relying on any "the record requires X", check WHICH
OBJECT the sentence is about and check that the cited line even exists.

**3. CITE STRINGS, NOT LINE NUMBERS.** Line numbers rot here and half the stale
citations in this repository are that. Quote the code and let the reader grep.

**4. FREEZE THE TREE WHILE A RENDER QUEUE RUNS.** Do not edit source while renders
are in flight. And make the runner report Blender's status, not the last command's —
`rc=$?` after a redirect reports `echo`'s status and will print rc=0 over three
aborted renders.

---

## Measuring

**5. RENDER IT, CROP IT, AND LOOK AT IT** — before and after every change. (canon
rule 28.) Every headline finding for five revisions running came from looking at an
image, and every one of them came through a clean VERIFY with all checks green.
VERIFY passing is not evidence that the thing looks right.

**6. MARK THE WINDOW AND LOOK AT IT BEFORE YOU COMPUTE A NUMBER, NOT AFTER.** A
measurement's window is part of the measurement (canon rule 8). Three dead windows
in one revision returned plausible percentages from boxes that were not on the part.

**7. A MEASUREMENT FROM THE WRONG ARTWORK STATE IS NOT A MEASUREMENT.** (canon rule
26.) Geometry transfers between the reference frames; PAINT AND ARTWORK DO NOT.
Check which state, not just which bus. Byte-identical duplicate files are not
corroboration.

**8. REPORT THE MEASUREMENT AGAINST THE REFERENCE, WITH ITS CEILING. NEVER A SCORE
YOU ASSIGNED YOURSELF, AND NEVER "READY."** Tell him what is fixed, what is still
wrong, and what you measured. "It cannot be recovered from what we hold" is a real
result — it turns a guess into a photograph request.

---

## Before you trust any instrument you built

**9. A CONTROL IS NOT FINISHED WHEN IT PASSES. IT IS FINISHED WHEN YOU HAVE WATCHED
IT FAIL ON THE DEFECT.** (canon rule 19.) A guard that CRASHES reports nothing. An
instrument that has never been wrong has never been tested (canon rule 20).

**10. NEVER PUT A FIGURE IN AN ACCEPTANCE TEST UNLESS YOU WATCHED IT PRINT.**
(canon rule 4.)

**11. A GUARD THAT DERIVES ITS THRESHOLD FROM THE SAME EXPRESSION IT CHECKS IS A
TAUTOLOGY.** (canon rule 32.) It must compare two INDEPENDENTLY OBTAINED
quantities: the built thing against the built thing it is fitted to.

**12. READ EACH PROBE'S OWN SUMMARY LINE, NEVER ITS EXIT CODE.** (canon rule 3.)

**13. A CLAIM IN PROSE IS NOT A GUARD, AND A CLAIM IN A SOURCE COMMENT IS NOT A
MEASUREMENT.** (canon rule 1.) Grepping for an object name is not a test for whether
a feature is built — lofted and swept features have no object name (canon rule 31).
Ask the mesh, or ask `STATE.md`.

---

## Running the revision

**14. GRADE THE BRIEF BEFORE YOU TRUST IT, AND PUT AN AGENT ON IT WHOSE ONLY JOB IS
TO REFUTE IT. DO NOT CLOSE UNTIL IT REPORTS.** Every brief for six revisions has
been wrong in single digits to low teens of places. This cuts both ways: the brief
is also where his rulings live.

**15. THREE THINGS IN THE SAME EDIT AS THE THING THEY ARE ABOUT.** Add the guard in
the same edit as the change (canon rule 12). Retract in the same revision you find
the error — in SPEC, in the source, and to him (canon rule 15). And FINISH WHAT YOU
DISPATCH: closing with one effort outstanding has cost a whole revision twice.

---

## Machine facts that are not rules but bite every session

- `lid_gen.py` IS NOT CALLED BY `build.py`. It is run by hand and its output is
  tracked and checksummed. Change it and regenerate, or the render silently uses
  the old texture.
- `out/` is NOT tracked and starts EMPTY on a clone. Render before quoting any
  probe that reads a frame.
- Do NOT fan out Blender. It is CPU-bound; two instances make both slower. Run
  renders sequentially from one script in the background, analyse in the foreground.
- Do not `git add -A` a directory a live agent is writing.
- `verify_clone.sh` needs a CLEAN tree — commit first, then verify. Do not edit the
  script to make a row pass. A re-base is allowed only with the cause NAMED and a
  companion row that makes the cause separately testable.
- Ask him as MULTIPLE CHOICE with the crop attached, using the question tool. Ask
  what a PHOTOGRAPH shows — he has never stood in the bus.

---

